<TopicPage slug="subjects/archaeology/foundational/what-archaeology-is">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Archaeology is the study of the human past through its material remains — everything
    people left behind, from million-year-old stone tools to last century's landfills and
    sweet wrappers. It is the only discipline with direct access to the entire span of
    human existence: writing covers roughly five thousand years, but humans have been
    making and discarding things for over three million, and for most of that time the
    objects are all we have. Where historians read texts, archaeologists read the ground —
    and the ground records things no text ever thought to mention: what ordinary people
    ate, how they built, where they were buried, what they threw away, how their houses
    faced each other. It is, in that sense, the history of everyone, reconstructed from
    everyone's rubbish.
  </p>
  <p>
    The discipline runs on a sobering rule: <em>excavation destroys the site</em>. Digging
    is controlled, recorded destruction — you cannot re-dig a layer — so the record taken
    must outlast the site itself, which makes recording standards, archives and publication
    as much a part of the science as trowels. Everything in this chapter follows from that
    fact: the obsession with context, the preference for survey over shovel, and the
    professional horror with which the field regards treasure hunting.
  </p>
  <p>
    A visitor should leave with three maps of the subject: what archaeology actually
    studies (and how inference from broken pots to economies works), how the discipline
    grew out of gentlemanly collecting into a science, and the varieties of practice — from
    underwater to industrial to the cultural resource management that most working
    archaeologists actually do.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the method, from survival to inference" widget="equationStep" config={methodsCfg} />

  <h2>What archaeology studies</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> first, the dinosaurs. Archaeologists study humans and
    human ancestors — fossil dinosaurs belong to palaeontology, a separate science.
    (The confusion is so universal that the discipline's outreach material opens with it.)
    Second, the treasure. The gold mask makes the museum poster, but the gold mask is
    evidence about metallurgy, trade and belief only because of <em>where it lay</em> — in
    what layer, with what objects, oriented how. That find-position, the provenance and
    association, is the science.
  </p>
  <p>
    The method follows from that principle. Before any digging, <em>survey</em> maps what
    is there: fieldwalking collects surface material ploughed up by farmers; geophysics
    images what is under the soil (resistivity surveys detect buried walls by their effect
    on soil moisture; magnetometry detects ditches, hearths and kilns by their
    magnetism); and airborne lidar strips forest canopy in software to reveal earthworks
    no surveyor ever walked. When excavation is justified, it proceeds by
    <em>stratigraphy</em>: layers accumulate oldest-first (a floor lies under the ashes
    that fell on it), and each layer is a unit of time containing a moment of a site's
    life. Recording is continuous — plans, sections, photographs, three-dimensional
    models — and the Harris matrix formalises the stack of cut-and-fill relationships into
    a site's sequence. Finally comes inference: finds alone are anecdotes, but
    <em>patterns</em> — the same pottery style across forty sites, animal bones sorted by
    cut across a town, house floors rebuilt in the same footprints for two centuries —
    are evidence of trade, diet, belief and social structure. The artefact is a datum;
    the pattern is the argument.
  </p>
  <p>
    The record itself is skewed, and honest archaeology begins by admitting it. Organic
    materials — wood, cloth, basketry — survive only in special conditions (waterlogged,
    frozen, very dry), so the surviving past over-represents stone, bone, pottery and
    fired clay; wealth over-represents itself (burials of elites dominate the spectacular
    finds); and behaviour that left no objects (speech, law, song) survives only as
    inferred from what did. Reading a biased record is the discipline's permanent craft —
    the formation processes that created the record (discard, burial, erosion, later
    digging) are themselves objects of study.
  </p>
  <p>
    Dating deserves its own note, because it is the discipline's structural spine and it
    runs on a toolkit of complementary clocks. <em>Radiocarbon</em> covers organic
    material back about 50,000 years — with a stated uncertainty, now resolved by
    calibration curves built from tree rings, since atmospheric carbon-14 has not been
    constant. <em>Dendrochronology</em>, tree-ring dating, is exact to the year where
    long regional sequences exist (timbers in buildings and bogs match ring patterns
    against a master chronology). <em>Typology and seriation</em> order artefact styles
    by their frequencies across layers — the classical method, still indispensable where
    no datable organics survive. Beyond the radiocarbon range, other clocks take over
    (potassium–argon and its successors for early hominin sites, luminescence for last-
    exposed sediments). Every good excavation reports several independent dates for the
    same layers, because the argument is strongest when disagreeing clocks are made to
    agree.
  </p>

  <h2>The history of the discipline</h2>
  <p>
    Archaeology grew out of <strong>antiquarianism</strong>: the gentlemen collectors of
    the seventeenth to nineteenth centuries who mapped barrows, recorded stone circles
    and filled cabinets with curiosities. Their recording instincts were mixed, their
    methods destructive (a barrow tunnelled in a weekend), and their questions
    genealogical and romantic — but they preserved sites and started the idea that
    artefacts could be arranged in order. The first scientific breakthrough was
    chronological: Christian Jürgensen Thomsen's <em>Three Age System</em>
    (1836), organising the Copenhagen museum's collection into Stone, Bronze and Iron
    Ages from find associations — the first evidence-based framework for prehistory.
    Fieldwork standards rose through the century: General Pitt-Rivers in England insisted
    on total, planned recording of every find's position ("the value of relics depends
    on their associations"), and Flinders Petrie brought the same discipline to Egypt.
    The era's great drama — Heinrich Schliemann's trench through the mound of Troy in the
    1870s — shows both faces at once: he found (by his identification) Homer's city and
    proved that Bronze Age civilisation lay beneath the classical world's feet, while
    digging straight through the very strata that made his finds meaningful, which later
    archaeologists spent decades re-reading. The profession's modern instinct — record
    everything, destroy nothing, publish or the excavation did not happen — was written
    partly in reaction to him.
  </p>
  <p>
    The twentieth century professionalised and then theorised the field. Culture-history
    mapped artefact styles onto peoples and migrations; Mortimer Wheeler and Kathleen
    Kenyon refined stratigraphic excavation into a rigorous, box-planned method. Then two
    revolutions remade the discipline. <strong>Radiocarbon dating</strong> (Willard Libby,
    1949) allowed organic material to be dated directly from its carbon-14 content —
    freeing prehistory from guesswork and rapidly overturning chronologies built on
    cross-dating. And <strong>processual archaeology</strong> (Lewis Binford, from the
    1960s) demanded that archaeology behave like science: state hypotheses about past
    systems — subsistence, exchange, settlement — and test them, treating the record as
    data about behaviour rather than a catalogue of styles. The
    <strong>post-processual</strong> response (Ian Hodder, from the 1980s) answered that
    past people were not atoms in a system but makers of meaning: symbols, agency,
    individual lives and the archaeologist's own position belong in the interpretation —
    an argument the field still conducts productively, usually by doing both.
  </p>

  <h2>Kinds of archaeology</h2>
  <p>
    The field's branches are defined by period, medium, method or purpose — and the
    spread is wider than most visitors expect. By period: <strong>prehistoric
    archaeology</strong> works where texts are absent (everything from the Palaeolithic
    to pre-contact America), <strong>historical archaeology</strong> works alongside
    documents (colonial sites, plantations, whaling stations — often testing what the
    documents claim), and <strong>classical and biblical archaeology</strong> engage the
    oldest literate traditions. By medium: <strong>underwater (maritime)
    archaeology</strong> excavates shipwrecks and drowned landscapes, where anaerobic
    mud preserves wood and cargo intact; <strong>industrial archaeology</strong> records
    mills, railways and factories, the material history of the very recent past;
    <strong>landscape archaeology</strong> studies whole regions — field systems,
    roads, boundaries — rather than single sites. By specialism: zooarchaeology (animal
    bones: diet, herding, butchery), archaeobotany (seeds and charcoal: crops, fuel,
    fields), bioarchaeology (human remains: health, trauma, ancestry, migration),
    geoarchaeology (soils and sediments: how sites formed), experimental archaeology
    (building the boats, smelting the iron, knapping the tools to test what was
    possible) and ethnoarchaeology (watching living societies make the record, to
    calibrate the inferences).
  </p>
  <p>
    By employment, the dominant kind is <strong>cultural resource management</strong>
    (CRM): the legally mandated survey, recording and mitigation of sites threatened by
    construction — most professional archaeologists spend most of their careers there,
    which makes the discipline, in practice, a regulated conversation between development
    and heritage. <strong>Public and community archaeology</strong> extends the same
    logic to people whose heritage is being dug.
  </p>
  <p>
    One famous excavation shows the whole method in a single story. In 1939, on a Suffolk
    estate, Basil Brown — a self-taught excavator working for landowner Edith Pretty —
    opened the ship-mound at Sutton Hoo and found, undisturbed, the burial chamber of an
    early Anglo-Saxon king: a 27-metre ship, helmet, gold buckle, Byzantine silver,
    Merovingian coins. Everything that makes it science rather than spectacle came from
    the associations: the coins dated the burial (to the early seventh century), the
    lack of a body raised the question (was it a cenotaph — a memorial over a grave lost
    to the river's acid soil, or a body that left nothing?), and the grave-goods'
    reach — Frankish coins, Swedish helmet parallels, Mediterranean silver — turned a
    treasure into an argument about a kingdom, its trade and its politics at the edge of
    written history. The finds went to the British Museum under the law of treasure
    (Pretty kept them, then gave them); the recording survived; the site is still being
    excavated. Modern CRM diggers work to exactly this standard, at roundabout sites.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The scientific frontier keeps enlarging the record itself. <em>Ancient DNA</em>,
    extracted from bones since the 2010s, now reconstructs whole population movements —
    the peopling of Europe in successive waves, the Bantu expansions — sometimes
    overturning decades of artefact-based migration stories. <em>Isotope analysis</em>
    reads chemistry rather than genes: teeth lock in the isotopic signature of the water
    and food of childhood, so an individual buried far from home can be identified as a
    first-generation migrant; bone collagen reports on diet. <em>Lidar</em> delivered the
    2018 surprise over Guatemala's Petén: tens of thousands of previously unknown Maya
    structures — causeways, terraces, whole districts — under forest that has hidden them
    since European contact, forcing upward revision of Maya population by millions.
    <em>Proteomics</em> now reads residue from pots (what was actually cooked in them),
    and environmental archaeology reconstructs whole climates from the sediments around
    a site. The artefact catalogue has become a laboratory.
  </p>
  <p>
    The interpretive and ethical questions, meanwhile, stay stubbornly human.
    Repatriation is the defining one: indigenous communities reclaiming ancestors and
    sacred objects (in the US under NAGPRA, 1990; in parallel regimes worldwide),
    museums confronting how collections were formed, and archaeologists negotiating
    between scientific interest and descendant communities' rights — increasingly, co-
    managed research rather than excavation-then-consultation. The antiquities trade
    remains the discipline's enemy at the border: conflict-looted material still moves
    through market channels, and every unprovenanced object is a context destroyed.
    Open questions cluster where the science meets meaning: how to hear ordinary and
    marginal people in records dominated by architecture and wealth, how to
    distinguish collapse from transformation in the record of dead cities, and what a
    discipline built on digging should do when the best evidence says — as it often
    now does — to leave some sites in the ground for methods not yet invented.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Renfrew &amp; Bahn, <em>Archaeology: Theories, Methods and Practice</em>; Harris, <em>Principles of Archaeological Stratigraphy</em>.</p>
